  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nMostly sunny skies earlier in the day allowed temperatures to \nwarm well into the 80s by early afternoon with increasing high \ncloud coverage helping to stall further warming, with highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s expected for inland areas. A weak lake \nbreeze has kept temperatures a bit cooler near the lake, in the \nmid 70s to around 80, making for a pleasant day for late July.\n\nWe are monitoring a wave currently moving across the SD/NE \nborder this afternoon that is expected to gradually drift east \ntoward the Upper Mississippi Valley tonight into Friday, \neventually becoming a cut-off low. An associated deepening \nsurface low and area of showers and embedded thunderstorms will \nmove across Iowa into Illinois during the day on Friday. The \nexpectation here locally is for (mainly light) rain to move in \nfrom west to east through the afternoon, with the potential for \npockets of higher rainfall rates favored during the evening \nhours through Saturday morning. While there remains a fair \namount of variability in placement of the highest rainfall \namounts (upwards of 2-3+ inches) amongst the various suite of \navailable guidance as well as from run to run, the signal for \nheavy rainfall in the local area has remained fairly persistent.\n\nThe slow moving nature of this system paired with anomalously \nhigh PWATs would be supportive of torrential rainfall rates. Any\nareas where repeated rainfall occurs over multiple hours Friday\nevening through early Saturday could lead to flooding, \nespecially in urban and poor drainage areas. Accordingly, have \ncoordinated with the Weather Prediction Center (WPC) to expand \nthe Level 2 of 4 threat of flooding to include most of the area,\nincluding the Chicago metro.\n\nConfidence in how quickly showers end Saturday remains on the \nlower side with the potential that showers persist south of \nI-80 through the evening/overnight hours before ending toward \nearly Sunday morning. Mainly dry conditions conditions are then \nfavored during the first part of next week with gradually \nincreasing temperatures back into the 80s by TUE-WED. An upper \nlevel wave and attendant surface front may swing through the \narea toward midweek bringing renewed shower/storm chances.\n\nPetr",
